Says, at debug, what a layer surface's first new buffer after a quiet
spell spent between its client's commit and the screen: how long niri
took to take it, which for a dmabuf is how long its fence took to
signal, what the output's redraw was doing then, when the frame
carrying it began to render and was queued, when it reached the
screen, the refreshes from the commit, and how fast the screen's
vblanks came since its last frame. A shell opening its
launcher after a quiet moment saw its frame reach the screen 4 to 7
refreshes after its swap at 120 Hz, yet niri's own frames after an
idle spell reach it within 2 (says-a-frames-steps-after-idle.patch),
and none of the slow openings was among them, so the time goes before
niri is asked to redraw. The times are by the monotonic clock
presentation feedback uses, so a client's own timings line up. A
surface counts as quiet once 100 ms have passed since its last new
buffer, so an animation says nothing. It adds no setting; the tty
backend alone says it, since only it knows when a frame reaches the
screen. An output keeps at most 8 waiting for a frame, dropping any
taken over a second before, and a render with no damage clears them.
The client's namespace is quoted and escaped, so it can't write a log
line of its own, and nothing is stamped unless niri logs at debug.

